        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "statement": "Fix an inertial frame of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] and identify its vector coordinates with $\\mathbb R^4$ and $\\eta_0=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. For a unit spatial vector $\\mathbf n_B$ and $\\chi\\in\\mathbb R$, define the boost $B_{\\mathbf n_B}(\\chi)$ by $B e_0=\\cosh\\chi\\,e_0+\\sinh\\chi\\,\\mathbf n_B$, $B\\mathbf n_B=\\sinh\\chi\\,e_0+\\cosh\\chi\\,\\mathbf n_B$, and $Bw=w$ for every $w$ Euclidean-orthogonal to $\\operatorname{span}(e_0,\\mathbf n_B)$. Every proper orthochronous linear Lorentz map $\\Lambda\\in SO^+(1,3)$, as defined in [[def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group]], admits $\\Lambda=B_{\\mathbf n_B}(\\chi)R_{\\mathbf n_R}(\\theta)$, where $R_{\\mathbf n_R}(\\theta)$ fixes the time axis and $\\mathbf n_R$, and sends $\\mathbf v_\\perp$ to $\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathbf v_\\perp+\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathbf n_R\\times\\mathbf v_\\perp$ on the orthogonal plane, where $\\mathbf v_\\parallel=(\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf n_R)\\mathbf n_R$ and $\\mathbf v_\\perp=\\mathbf v-\\mathbf v_\\parallel$; the cross product is [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]. If $\\Lambda e_0=e_0$, choose $\\chi=0$ and $R_{\\mathbf n_R}(\\theta)=\\Lambda$.",
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 4, Lorentz group generators",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qft.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 155-page author-hosted notes; Chapter 4 §§4.1–4.2, Lorentz generators, rotations and boosts."
            },
