          "def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series": "mathematical-premise",
          "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-rational-box-step-functions-form-a-countable-dense-subset-of-l-p-of-rn": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and use the definitions in [[def-qft-dirac-projections]]. For every $\\mu\\ge0$ and almost every $\\mathbf k$, the displayed Pauli blocks satisfy $h_D(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger=h_D(\\mathbf k)$, $h_D(\\mathbf k)^2=\\omega(\\mathbf k)^2I_4$, and $P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)$ are complementary orthogonal projections of rank two. The ranges $\\mathcal H_\\pm$ are closed separable Hilbert subspaces of $\\mathcal H_D$, and $h_D$ is self-adjoint on $D(h_D)=\\{u:\\omega u\\in\\mathcal H_D\\}$. The matrices also satisfy $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$ and $\\gamma^{0\\dagger}=\\gamma^0$, $\\gamma^{j\\dagger}=-\\gamma^j$.\n\nFor a unit spatial vector $\\mathbf n$, put $\\Sigma_{\\mathbf n}=\\operatorname{diag}(\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\cdot\\mathbf n,\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\cdot\\mathbf n)$, $S_R(\\theta)=\\cos(\\theta/2)I_4-i\\sin(\\theta/2)\\Sigma_{\\mathbf n}$, and $S_B(\\chi)=\\cosh(\\chi/2)I_4+\\sinh(\\chi/2)\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf n$. With $R_{\\mathbf n}(\\theta)$ the rotation $\\mathbf v_\\parallel+\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathbf v_\\perp+\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathbf n\\times\\mathbf v_\\perp$ using [[def-cross-product-in-r3]], with $\\mathbf v_\\parallel=(\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf n)\\mathbf n$ and $\\mathbf v_\\perp=\\mathbf v-\\mathbf v_\\parallel$, and $B_{\\mathbf n}(\\chi)$ the boost defined in [[lem-qft-lorentz-boost-rotation-factorization]], these matrices satisfy $S_R\\Gamma(p)S_R^{-1}=\\Gamma(R_{\\mathbf n}(\\theta)p)$, $S_B\\Gamma(p)S_B^{-1}=\\Gamma(B_{\\mathbf n}(\\chi)p)$, and $S_R\\beta S_R^\\dagger=S_B\\beta S_B^\\dagger=\\beta$.\n\nFor each real $t$, $W_D(t,\\mathbf k)$ of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]] is unitary, satisfies $W_D(t+s,\\mathbf k)=W_D(t,\\mathbf k)W_D(s,\\mathbf k)$, and obeys $\\partial_tW_D(t,\\mathbf k)=i h_D(\\mathbf k)W_D(t,\\mathbf k)$.",
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 4, the Dirac equation and plane-wave solutions",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qft.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 155-page author-hosted notes; Chapter 4, Clifford algebra, explicit Dirac matrices and on-shell spinor solutions."
            },
            {
              "title": "Niklas Beisert, Quantum Field Theory I, ETH Zürich 2025 HS complete lecture notes",
              "url": "https://people.phys.ethz.ch/~nbeisert/lectures/QFT1-25HS-Notes.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 194-page official course notes; Chapter 5 §§5.1, 5.2, 5.4–5.6, printed 5.1–5.6 and 5.10–5.18, for Clifford algebra, spinor covariance, Hamiltonian form and fermionic quantization. The exact local conventions and domains are proved in the library."
            }
          ]
        }
