          "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, and use [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]], and the antisymmetric Fock and CAR conventions of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]] and [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. For $F\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C^4)$, define\n$$\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}F(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x,\\qquad u_F(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)\\,dt.$$\nThen $u_F\\in\\mathcal H_D$, continuously in the Schwartz topology, and\n$$\\Psi(F)=c(P_+u_F)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-u_F})$$\ndefines a bounded operator on $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-})$, antilinear in $F$. It satisfies\n$$\\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)\\}=0,\\qquad \\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)^\\dagger\\}=\\langle u_F,u_G\\rangle_{\\mathcal H_D}I.$$\nThe positive and negative contraction kernels, defined as tempered distributions by their Fourier integrals, are\n$$C_+(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{-i(\\omega\\tau-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_+(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3},\\qquad C_-(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{+i(\\omega\\tau+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_-(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}.$$\nTheir tested pairings give respectively $\\langle P_+u_F,P_+u_G\\rangle$ and $\\langle P_-u_F,P_-u_G\\rangle$; their sum is the anticommutator kernel $R$ below.\nIts tested free equation is $u_{(i\\partial_0-h_D(-i\\nabla))F}=0$. For spatial Schwartz spinors $u,v$, the equal-time field $\\Psi_t(u)=c(P_+W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u})$ satisfies $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)^\\dagger\\}=\\int u(\\mathbf x)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x\\,I$ and $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)\\}=0$.\n\nThe spacetime anticommutator kernel is\n$$R(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}W_D(-\\tau,\\mathbf k)e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}=\\partial_\\tau\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x)I_4-i h_D(-i\\nabla)\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x),$$\nwhere $\\Delta_\\mu$ is the scalar causal commutator distribution of [[lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator]]. Thus the kernel is supported in the closed causal cone. Spinor tests with compact spacelike-separated supports have vanishing anticommutators with one another and with each other's adjoints. Consequently, even polynomials in the bounded smeared fields commute across spacelike-separated regions. These are mathematical properties of the specified free-field construction.",
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 5, quantizing the Dirac field",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qft.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 155-page author-hosted notes; Chapter 5 §§5.2–5.4, mode expansion, canonical anticommutation relations, anticommutator kernel and spacelike locality."
            },
            {
              "title": "Niklas Beisert, Quantum Field Theory I, ETH Zürich 2025 HS complete lecture notes",
              "url": "https://people.phys.ethz.ch/~nbeisert/lectures/QFT1-25HS-Notes.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 194-page official course notes; Chapter 5 §§5.1, 5.2, 5.4–5.6, printed 5.1–5.6 and 5.10–5.18, for Clifford algebra, spinor covariance, Hamiltonian form and fermionic quantization. The exact local conventions and domains are proved in the library."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
